    "type": "project",
    "title": "Automated Weather Pipeline for Dhaka",
    "thumbnail": "images/weather_dashboard.png",
    "summary": "Cloud-native weather intelligence for Dhaka, orchestrated by Prefect, stored on S3 and visualized with Streamlit.",
    "content": "<div style=\"text-align: justify;\"><p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\">The concept was straightforward: <b>Pull weather data from Open-Meteo</b> \u2192 <b>transform and clean it</b> \u2192 <b>store as Parquet on S3</b> \u2192 <b>serve it through a Streamlit</b> + <b>Plotly dashboard</b> \u2192 <b>orchestrated with Airflow and Prefect.</b></p>\n<p class=\"font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\" style=\"text-align: left;\">Simple on paper. Humbling in practice.</p></div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/architecture.jpg\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"><span style=\"font-size: 0.95rem;\"></span></div><div style=\"text-align: justify;\"><div>Extraction was the easiest part as the Open-Meteo API is generous and well-documented. The real work was in the transform layer. Units matter more than you think. I'm based in Bangladesh. My stakeholders (okay, it's just me, but still) think in Celsius and km/h. The API hands you data in various units depending on what you ask for. So the transformation layer standardizes everything. Celsius for temperature, km/h for wind speed, with a careful normalization pass to handle edge cases like missing hourly readings. Then there's timezones. APIs hand you UTC by default but I needed everything converted to Asia/Dhaka, which Python's <i><b>zoneinfo</b></i> handled cleanly.</div><div><br></div><div>I could've stored the data as CSV which was fine, but Parquet gives you columnar storage, efficient compression and schema enforcement out of the box. Since I'm dumping data to <b><i>S3</i></b> and reading it back for analysis, that efficiency compounds fast.<br><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/s3_bucket.png\" style=\"font-size: 0.95rem; max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div></div><div>And honestly? Pushing data to <i><b>S3</b></i> for the first time hits different. It's not some dummy project on your laptop anymore. It feels like you're building actual infrastructure, real world stuff. Once the ETL was done, I used <i><b>boto3</b></i> and <i><b>pandas</b></i> to pull the data directly into <b><i>Streamlit</i></b>, then handed it off to <b><i>Plotly</i></b> for the chart visuals.</div><div><br></div></div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/weather_dashboard.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"><b></b></div><div style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b><b>Github link:</b>&nbsp;</b><a href=\"https://github.com/MahirTB/dhaka-weather-pipeline\" style=\"font-weight: bold;\" target=\"_blank;\">https://github.com/MahirTB/dhaka-weather-pipeline</a></div><div style=\"text-align: justify;\"><b><b>See the dashboard:</b>&nbsp;<a href=\"https://dhakaweatherinsight.streamlit.app/\" target=\"_blank;\"><b>https://dhakaweatherinsight.streamlit.app/</b></a></b></div>",
    "savedAt": "2026-04-04T03:16:18.146Z",
    "id": 4,
    "date": "2025-11-23"
  },
  {
    "type": "project",
    "title": "AdventureWorks Sales Performance Dashboard",
    "summary": "Interactive sales intelligence for AdventureWorks' Internet Sales channel, modeled in SQL Server and built with Power BI and DAX.",
    "thumbnail": "images/adv_pbi_final.png",
    "content": "I wanted to build a proper Power BI project using a more realistic dataset than a single CSV, so I picked Microsoft's AdventureWorksDW sample database instead of something from Kaggle. It came as a SQL install script bundled with CSV files, so I actually had to set up SQL Server Express and SQL Server Management Studio myself, then run the script. I ran into a few hiccups doing that, like fixing hardcoded file paths in the script and sorting out a local SSL certificate issue, just to get the database created in the first place.<div><br></div><div><img src=\"images/ssms_db.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0;\"></div><div>The full AdventureWorksDW schema covers sales, finance, HR, call center, and inventory all in one warehouse, which is a lot more than I actually needed. I scoped it down to just the Internet Sales tables, keeping Product, Customer, Geography, Date, Currency and Sales Territory, since importing everything would've made the model messy and unfocused.</div><div><br></div><div><img src=\"images/adv_pbi_flat_table.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0;\"></div><div>One change I made early on was flattening the product hierarchy. By default, Product, Subcategory, and Category are three separate linked tables, which makes sense for a database but adds complexity you don't really need for reporting. I merged the category and subcategory names directly into the Product table using Power Query and got rid of the two extra tables afterward.</div><div><br></div><div>For the DAX side, I built year over year growth measures using CALCULATE with SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR, and used DIVIDE instead of a plain division sign so it wouldn't break when a value happened to be zero. For Average Order Value, I had to use DISTINCTCOUNT on the order number instead of just counting rows, since one order can have multiple product lines in it. For margins, I used SUMX to multiply quantity and cost row by row, since that number doesn't exist anywhere as a single column on its own. I also left missing cost values as blank instead of filling them with zero, since a zero cost would make a product look way more profitable than it actually is.</div><div style=\"text-align: center; \"><img src=\"data:image/png;base64,\" alt=\"\"></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">For the Best and Worst Performing Category and Country cards, I wrote measures using ADDCOLUMNS with MAXX and MINX to dynamically find whichever one has the highest or lowest sales at any given time, so it updates automatically depending on whatever year someone filters to.</div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">I also added a toggle that switches the main trend chart between Revenue and Profit using Power BI's field parameters, along with a dynamic chart title that changes its text depending on which one is currently selected.</div><div style=\"text-align: left;\"><br></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">One decision I'm glad I made was building the KPI cards without any third-party visuals. I wanted the value, the trend arrow, and the percentage change all in one card, similar to some dashboards I'd seen online, but I didn't want to rely on custom visuals for something that basic. So I built each card by layering a background shape, a card visual for the number, a small line chart with all its axes and gridlines stripped out to act as a sparkline, and a second card for the percentage change, then grouped them together as one unit. It took more effort than just installing a visual from AppSource, but it meant I actually understood every piece of what I was building instead of depending on someone else's plugin.</div><div style=\"text-align: left;\"><img src=\"images/adv_pbi_final.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0;\"></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\"><img src=\"images/adv_pbi_final_pg2.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin: 10px 0;\"></div>",
    "savedAt": "2026-09-19T09:01:04.777Z",
    "id": 3,
    "date": "2024-04-19"
  },
  {
    "type": "project",
    "title": "IDA Loan Report",
    "thumbnail": "images/ida-dashboard.png",
    "summary": "Explored the IDA dataset using SQL and built a simple Power BI dashboard",
    "content": "Sourced the 'IDA Statement of Credits and Grants' dataset from the World Bank website. I modeled a relational database and loaded the raw data into SQL Server Management Studio (SSMS) as two distinct tables.<div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/ida-intro.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">Conducted initial exploratory data analysis to understand the database schema and table contents.</div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/ida_db_explore.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">Executed SQL JOIN operations to consolidate the tables and establish a comprehensive view of the dataset.</div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/ida-q3.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">During data validation, I identified an unexpected cardinality issue. I initially assumed a 1:1 relationship between Project ID and Credit Number. However, querying the row counts revealed a one-to-many relationship, where a single Project ID was associated with multiple Credit Numbers.</div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/ida_check.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">To handle the resulting duplicates and isolate unique records for accurate analysis, I utilized Common Table Expressions (CTEs).</div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/ida-unq.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">Aggregated the financial data to analyze the overall distribution of loans on a country-by-country basis.</div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/ida-q-by-country.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">Calculated the total number of IDA-granted projects allocated to each respective country.</div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/ida-projectcount_2.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">Finally, designed and developed an interactive Power BI dashboard to visualize these metrics and deliver actionable insights.</div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/ida-dashboard.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div>",
    "savedAt": "2026-02-17T17:06:34.110Z",
    "id": 2,
    "date": "2023-12-27"
  },
  {
    "type": "project",
    "title": "Covid-19 Insights - My First Data Analytics Project",
    "thumbnail": "images/covid-dashboard.png",
    "summary": "This is my first data analytics project where I've used PowerBI for the first tiime",
    "content": "<div>I downloaded a COVID dataset from Kaggle, and it turned out to be quite extensive. Most columns were fine, but there were null values in some. I decided to clean it up by removing those null columns and started my analysis. While getting total cases and total deaths was straightforward using Power BI, calculating metrics like vaccinations per hundred people and death rates proved to be a bit tricky. The nature of the dataset was such that it provided daily updates, resulting in numerous duplicates. To streamline the data, I undertook manipulations to derive the total population and the number of people vaccinated for each country.</div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/covid-script.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\"><div><br></div><div>After obtaining the data, I created a new sheet where I calculated the vaccination percentage of people for each country and later used it for visualization.</div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/popvac sheet.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\"><br></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">Next up, I needed to determine the death rate (how many people are dying per hundred cases) for each country. Using DAX, I created a new table specifically to calculate the death rate.</div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/death-ratio-dax.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\"><br></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">Now that I have all the metrics, I dived into visualization using Power BI and made a dynamic dashboard which shows all the key metrics regarding the pandemic.</div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/covid-dashboard.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><br></div><div style=\"text-align: left;\">Now, if I want to view the data specific to my country, Bangladesh, I can use the country filter. Additionally, I have the flexibility to explore data for any particular year as needed.</div><div style=\"text-align: center;\"><img src=\"images/covid-bd.png\" style=\"max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px; margin-bottom: 10px;\"></div></div>",
    "savedAt": "2026-01-30T13:46:06.973Z",
    "id": 1,
    "date": "2023-11-12"
  }
]